      "id": 1,
      "text": "Godwin was named after pioneer settler Isaac William Godwin in the early 1900s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Godwin%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1878 Godwin convinced the Wilmington & Weldon Railroad to build a station near his home.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://mccog.org/member-municipalities.asp?m=4",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s first Presbyterian church began as a Sunday school in 1889, burned in 1934, and later shared space with the Baptist church.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Godwin%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The new Godwin Presbyterian building, completed in 1937, hosts an annual homecoming each October.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Godwin%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Godwin sits in northeastern Cumberland County, with Route 301 passing through it toward Dunn and Fayetteville.",
